arXiv:2307.08830v3 [math.AG] 25 Oct 2024</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of <math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>S.C. was supported by a Hermann-Weyl-Instructorship from the Forschungsinstitut für Mathematik at ETH Zürich. This research was partially conducted during the period H.L. served as a Clay Research Fellow and as a guest of the Forschungsinstitut für Mathematik at ETH Zürich. S.P. was supported in part by NSF grants DMS–2302475 and DMS–2053261. </span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Samir Canning
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hannah Larson
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sam Payne
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study collections of subrings of <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> that are closed under the tautological operations that map cohomology classes on moduli spaces of smaller dimension to those on moduli spaces of larger dimension and contain the tautological subrings. Such extensions of tautological rings are well-suited for inductive arguments and flexible enough for a wide range of applications. In particular, we confirm predictions of Chenevier and Lannes for the <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representations and Hodge structures that appear in <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=13</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math>. We also show that <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by tautological classes for all <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming a prediction of Arbarello and Cornalba from the 1990s. In order to establish the final base cases needed for the inductive proofs of our main results, we use Mukai’s construction of canonically embedded pentagonal curves of genus 7 as linear sections of an orthogonal Grassmannian and a decomposition of the diagonal to show that the pure weight cohomology of <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by algebraic cycle classes, for <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The moduli spaces of stable curves <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth and proper over the integers, and this implies strong restrictions on the motivic structures, such as <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representations, that can appear in <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Widely believed conjectures regarding analytic continuations and functional equations for <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-functions lead to precise predictions, by Chenevier and Lannes, about which such structures can appear in degrees less than or equal to 22 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Theorem F]</cite>. These predictions are consistent with all previously known results on <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Recent work inspired by these predictions confirms their correctness in all degrees less than or equal to 12 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Here we introduce new methods to systematically study the motivic structures in <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;12</annotation></semantics></math> and confirm these predictions in degrees <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout, we write <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for the rational singular cohomology of a scheme or Deligne-Mumford stack <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> endowed with its associated Hodge structure or <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representation and <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(X)^{\mathrm{ss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(X)^{\mathrm{ss}}</annotation></semantics></math> for its semi-simplification. Let <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}:=H^{2}(\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}:=H^{2}(\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}:=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}:=H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{14}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>14</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{14}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{7}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, for <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> confirms the predictions of Chenevier and Lannes for motivic weights <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 15</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the Hodge structure on the cohomology of a smooth and proper Deligne–Mumford stack such as <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is semi-simple, so the semi-simplification in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> is relevant only when considering <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> uses the inductive structure of the boundary of the moduli space and the maps induced by tautological morphisms between moduli spaces, as do the proofs of the precursor results mentioned above. Recall that the collection of tautological rings <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\subset H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\subset H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the smallest collection of subrings that is closed under pushforward and pullback for the tautological morphisms induced by gluing, forgetting, and permuting marked points. For many inductive arguments, including those used here, it suffices to consider the operations that produce cohomology classes on moduli spaces of larger dimension from those on moduli spaces of smaller dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semi-tautological extension</em> (STE) is a collection of subrings <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> that contains the tautological subrings <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and is closed under pullback by forgetting and permuting marked points and under pushforward for gluing marked points.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Examples of STEs include the trivial extension <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the full cohomology rings <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the collection of subrings generated by algebraic cycle classes. Not every STE is closed under the additional tautological operations induced by push-forward for forgetting marked points and pullback for gluing marked points. However, the main examples we study here are indeed closed under all of the tautological operations. See Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that any intersection of STEs is an STE. An STE is <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finitely generated</em> if it is the smallest STE that contains a given finite subset (or, equivalently, the union of finitely many <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector subspaces) of <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\coprod_{g,n}H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\coprod_{g,n}H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. A finitely generated STE is suitable for combinatorial study via algebraic operations on decorated graphs. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> for discussions of the graphical algebra underlying the tautological ring, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> for applications of such operadic methods, with not necessarily tautological decorations, to the weight spectral sequence for <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Every STE that we consider is <em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">motivic</em>, meaning that <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\subset H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\subset H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a sub-Hodge structure and its base change to <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is preserved by the Galois action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any fixed degree <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the STE generated by</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{H^{k^{\prime}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}):k^{\prime}\leq k,g^{\prime}&lt;\tfrac{3}{2}k^{\prime}+1,\ n^{\prime}\leq k^{\prime},\ 4g^{\prime}-4+n^{\prime}\geq k^{\prime}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn> 4</mn><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{H^{k^{\prime}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}):k^{\prime}\leq k,g^{\prime}&lt;\tfrac{3}{2}k^{\prime}+1,\ n^{\prime}\leq k^{\prime},\ 4g^{\prime}-4+n^{\prime}\geq k^{\prime}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contains <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, there is a finitely generated STE that contains <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, there are only finitely many isomorphism classes of simple Hodge structures (respectively, <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representations) in <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{g,n}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{g,n}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We developed the notion of STEs to study nontrivial extensions of tautological rings, such as the STE generated by <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>, but the same methods also yield new results on the tautological ring itself. We apply the explicit bounds in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, together with new tools and results for small <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to prove the following. At the level of <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces, we identify <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2d_{g,n}-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2d_{g,n}-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{g,n}:=3g-3+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{g,n}:=3g-3+n</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of <math id="S1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, when <math id="S1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is an STE we write <math id="S1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space <math id="S1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2d_{g,n}-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2d_{g,n}-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tautological ring <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> contains</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}),</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}),</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}),</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for even </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 14</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for all </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The STE generated by <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The STE generated by <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>(<a href="#S1.I1.i1" title="item 1 ‣ Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) confirms a prediction of Arbarello and Cornalba from the 1990s; they proposed that their inductive method used to prove that <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological should also apply in degree 4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, p. 1]</cite>. Shortly thereafter, Polito confirmed that <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, but the general case remained open until now.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>(<a href="#S1.I1.i3" title="item 3 ‣ Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) implies that <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{k/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{k/2}</annotation></semantics></math> for even <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 14</annotation></semantics></math>. The work of Chenevier and Lannes predicts that <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> should also be isomorphic to <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{k/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{k/2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=16,18,20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=16,18,20</annotation></semantics></math>. The Hodge and Tate conjectures then predict that these groups are generated by algebraic cycle classes. However, generation by algebraic cycle classes is an open problem except in the cases where <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is known to be generated by tautological cycle classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">By Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a>, <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> lie in STEs generated by cohomology from genus <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> moduli spaces. On the other hand, <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{17}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>17</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{17}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> requires genus <math id="S1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> data because <math id="S1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{17}H^{17}(\mathcal{M}_{2,14})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>17</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>17</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{17}H^{17}(\mathcal{M}_{2,14})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We note that any STE is top-heavy, in the sense that <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\leq\dim S_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\leq\dim S_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq\dim\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>). Moreover, the dimensions in even and odd degrees are unimodal. This is because any STE contains <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence contains an ample class. Multiplication by a suitable power of the ample class gives an injection from <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">One could show that <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by tautological cycles in the remaining cases covered by Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>(<a href="#S1.I1.i3" title="item 3 ‣ Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) by showing that the pairing on <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\times RH_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\times RH_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is perfect, e.g., using <span id="S1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">admcycles</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. However, computational complexity prevents meaningful progress by brute force. Note that Petersen and Tommasi showed that this pairing is not perfect in general for <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 22</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. Graber and Pandharipande had previously shown that <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{22}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,20})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>22</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{22}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,20})</annotation></semantics></math> contains an algebraic cycle class that is not tautological <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.8</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tautological ring <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>
for even <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 20</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The results above show that Conjecture <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Conjecture 1.8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a> is true for <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 14</annotation></semantics></math>, the conjecture is true if and only if the pairing <math id="S1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\times RH_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\times RH_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is perfect. The examples of Graber–Pandharipande and Petersen–Tommasi show that the conjectured bound of <math id="S1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 20</annotation></semantics></math> is the best possible.
As further evidence for Conjecture <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Conjecture 1.8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a>, we note that
the Arbarello–Cornalba induction together with known base cases implies the vanishing of <math id="S1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{16,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{16,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{18,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{18,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, as recently observed by Fontanari <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">The inductive arguments used to study <math id="S1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> rely on understanding base cases <math id="S1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k^{\prime}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k^{\prime}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are small relative to <math id="S1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S1.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> grows, more base cases are needed. With the exception of <math id="S1.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>, all base cases required for previous work with <math id="S1.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> have been pure Hodge–Tate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Substantial work went into establishing these base cases via point counts and other methods. When <math id="S1.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 13</annotation></semantics></math>, the problem becomes fundamentally more difficult, as an increasing number of the required base cases are <em id="S1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> pure Hodge–Tate. In particular, previous techniques for handling base cases do not apply.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">The advances presented here depend on a new technique for controlling the pure weight cohomology of <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. A space <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the <em id="S1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Chow–Künneth generation Property (CKgP)</em> if the tensor product map on Chow groups <math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}(X)\otimes A_{*}(Y)\to A_{*}(X\times Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}(X)\otimes A_{*}(Y)\to A_{*}(X\times Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective for all <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, proper, and has the CKgP, then the cycle class map is an isomorphism. However, in several of the base cases needed for our arguments, the smooth and proper moduli space <math id="S1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has odd cohomology and hence does not have the CKgP. Nevertheless, we show that the open moduli spaces <math id="S1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> do have the CKgP for the relevant pairs <math id="S1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. To apply this in the proof of our main results, the key new technical statement is Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, which says that if <math id="S1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and has the CKgP, then <math id="S1.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraic. This extension of the aforementioned result on the cycle class map to spaces that are not necessarily proper is essential for controlling the motivic structures that appear in <math id="S1.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 13</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, using that <math id="S1.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{3,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{3,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP for <math id="S1.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite>, we determine the Hodge structures and Galois representations that appear in <math id="S1.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S1.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.9</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial in <math id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Bergström and Faber recently used point counting techniques to compute the cohomology of <math id="S1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,n}</annotation></semantics></math> as an <math id="S1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Galois representation for <math id="S1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 14</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 9</annotation></semantics></math>, these computations are conditional on the assumption that the only <math id="S1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representations appearing are those from the list of Chenevier and Lannes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm9" title="Theorem 1.9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.9</span></a> unconditionally confirms the calculations of Bergström and Faber for <math id="S1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">In order to prove Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>(<a href="#S1.I1.i3" title="item 3 ‣ Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), and <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a>, our inductive arguments require several base cases beyond what was already in the literature. In particular, we prove the following results in genus <math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>, which are also of independent interest.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.10</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, the moduli space <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{7,n})=A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{7,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{7,n})=A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{7,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the subring of the Chow ring <math id="S1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by tautological cycle classes. Previous results proving that <math id="S1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and <math id="S1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for small <math id="S1.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> have primarily relied on corresponding results for Hurwitz spaces with marked points <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Unfortunately, the numerics for degree <math id="S1.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> covers prevented this technique from working with marked points. Here, we take a new approach to the pentagonal locus, using a modification of Mukai’s construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> that includes markings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p20" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgements.</span> We thank Jonas Bergström, Carel Faber, Rahul Pandharipande, Dan Petersen, Kartik Prasanna, Burt Totaro, and Thomas Willwacher for helpful conversations related to this work. We especially thank Dan Petersen for his comments that led to an improved version of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we establish notation and terminology that we will use throughout the paper and discuss a few basic examples of STEs. We also recall previously known facts about the cohomology groups of moduli spaces, especially in genus 0, 1, and 2, that will be used in the base cases of our inductive arguments.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Preliminaries on STEs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that an STE is, by definition, closed under the tautological operations given by pushing forward from the boundary or pulling back from moduli spaces with fewer marked points. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the normalization of the boundary.
The sequence</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k-2}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k-2}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is right exact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i}\colon\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i}\colon\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the tautological morphism forgetting the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th marking and let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{k}_{g,n}:=\pi_{1}^{*}W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n-1})+\cdots+\pi_{n}^{*}W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n-1})\subset W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{k}_{g,n}:=\pi_{1}^{*}W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n-1})+\cdots+\pi_{n}^{*}W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n-1})\subset W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The following lemma is a cohomological analogue of the “filling criteria” in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Section 4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the partial order in which <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\prec(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≺</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\prec(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}\leq g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\neq(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\neq(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. The moduli space <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is stratified according to the topological types of stable curves, and each stratum of the boundary is a finite quotient of a product of moduli spaces <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\prec(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≺</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\prec(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that we write <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{g,n}:=3g-3+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{g,n}:=3g-3+n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be an STE and let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. If the canonical map</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{k^{\prime}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})\to W_{k^{\prime}}H^{k^{\prime}}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})/\big(\Phi^{k^{\prime}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}+RH^{k^{\prime}}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})\big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{k^{\prime}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})\to W_{k^{\prime}}H^{k^{\prime}}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})/\big(\Phi^{k^{\prime}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}+RH^{k^{\prime}}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is surjective for <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime},k^{\prime})=(g,n,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime},k^{\prime})=(g,n,k)</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime},k^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime},k^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\prec(g,n),\qquad 2d_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}-k^{\prime}\leq 2d_{g,n}-k\qquad\text{and}\qquad k^{\prime}\leq k-2" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≺</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\prec(g,n),\qquad 2d_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}-k^{\prime}\leq 2d_{g,n}-k\qquad\text{and}\qquad k^{\prime}\leq k-2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is by induction on <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the diagram</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.E3.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="76.58" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-38.29px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 395.14 76.58" width="395.14"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,76.58) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(197.57,0) translate(0,38.29)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -197.57 -29.26)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 76.57)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.81)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 1.67 0) translate(34.77,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -28.81 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.16em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.21" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="43.78"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E3.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces S^{k-2}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces S^{k-2}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 74.55 0) translate(71.18,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -32.01 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.63em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.71" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="50.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E3.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 67.55)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(36.44,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -30.48 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.41em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.21" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="47.13"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E3.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces H^{k-2}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces H^{k-2}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 72.88 0) translate(72.85,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -33.68 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.87em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.71" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="53.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E3.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 185.37 0) translate(86.63,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -47.46 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.86em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.71" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="81.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E3.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 325.42 0) translate(51.47,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.3 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E3.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0.}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -161.13 12.68 L -161.13 -11.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -161.13 -11.9)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -157.87 -1.84)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.52em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="7.19"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E3.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -126.08 24.94 L -90.64 24.94"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -90.36 24.94)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -51.84 12.18 L -51.84 -11.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -51.84 -11.9)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -48.59 -2.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.45em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.27"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E3.m1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\beta}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -20.1 12.18 L 40 -11.97"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.9279 -0.37283 0.37283 0.9279 40.26 -12.07)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 13.46 5.14)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.48em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.65"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E3.m1.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\phi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -124.41 -25.81 L -92.31 -25.81"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -92.03 -25.81)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -11.93 -25.81 L 20.18 -25.81"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.45 -25.81)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 128.12 -25.81 L 160.22 -25.81"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 160.5 -25.81)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Here, we extend <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the natural way, by summing over components, using the Künneth formula, and taking invariants under automorphisms of the dual graph. We claim that <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. By the Künneth formula, the domain of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of tensor products of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\ell}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\gamma,\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\ell}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\gamma,\nu})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\leq k-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\leq k-2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,\nu)\prec(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≺</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,\nu)\prec(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, by considering dimensions of the cycles involved, we must also have <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d_{\gamma,\nu}-\ell\leq 2d_{g,n}-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d_{\gamma,\nu}-\ell\leq 2d_{g,n}-k</annotation></semantics></math>. This is because we must have <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-2-\ell\leq 2(d_{g,n}-1-d_{\gamma,\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-2-\ell\leq 2(d_{g,n}-1-d_{\gamma,\nu})</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the degree of the other Künneth component does not exceed its real dimension. Now, suppose we are given <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime},k^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime},k^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\prec(\gamma,\nu),\qquad 2d_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}-k^{\prime}\leq 2d_{\gamma,\nu}-\ell\qquad\text{and}\qquad k^{\prime}\leq\ell-2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≺</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>and</mtext><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\prec(\gamma,\nu),\qquad 2d_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}-k^{\prime}\leq 2d_{\gamma,\nu}-\ell\qquad\text{and}\qquad k^{\prime}\leq\ell-2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime},k^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime},k^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Preliminaries on STEs ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), so <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\ell}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\gamma,\nu})=H^{\ell}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\gamma,\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\ell}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\gamma,\nu})=H^{\ell}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\gamma,\nu})</annotation></semantics></math> by induction. This proves the claim.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, by induction we have <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n-1})=S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n-1})=S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, so the image of</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i}^{*}\colon H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n-1})\to H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i}^{*}\colon H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n-1})\to H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">is contained in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{k}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{k}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the image of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. The tautological classes <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\subseteq W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\subseteq W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> are also contained in the image of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> by definition.
Thus, the surjectivity of</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/\left(\Phi^{k}_{g,n}+RH^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/\left(\Phi^{k}_{g,n}+RH^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">implies that <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective. Hence, <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is also surjective, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In order to apply Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Preliminaries on STEs ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, we need results that help us understand generators for <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/\left(\Phi^{k}_{g,n}+RH^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/\left(\Phi^{k}_{g,n}+RH^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the topic of Sections <a href="#S3" title="3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4" title="4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the only nonvanishing pure cohomology is <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{0}(\mathcal{M}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{0}(\mathcal{M}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Below, we present the complete classification of pure cohomology in genus <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is due to Getzler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. In genus <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, we present a classification in low cohomological degree, following Petersen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.1. </span>Genus 1</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following statement appeared in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Proposition 7]</cite>; the proof there is omitted. Here, we include the proof (and corrected statement), which was explained to us by Dan Petersen. Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> structure associated to the space of cusp forms of weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon E\to\mathcal{M}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon E\to\mathcal{M}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal curve and let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the local system <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the Eichler–Shimura correspondence, we have <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{k+1}=W_{k}H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes{k-1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{k+1}=W_{k}H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes{k-1}})</annotation></semantics></math>. We will see that the latter is identified with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,k})</annotation></semantics></math>. More generally, we have the following. Given a partition <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> be the associated Specht module representation of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq k," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq k,</annotation></semantics></math> a basis for
<math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n-1}{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n-1}{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> pullbacks from <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,A})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> runs over all subsets of <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\in A</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently,
there is an <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})\cong\mathsf{S}_{k+1}\otimes V_{n-k+1,1^{k-1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})\cong\mathsf{S}_{k+1}\otimes V_{n-k+1,1^{k-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon E\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon E\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the universal elliptic curve, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\colon\mathcal{M}_{1,1}\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\colon\mathcal{M}_{1,1}\to E</annotation></semantics></math> the section. Associated to the open embedding <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1,n}\hookrightarrow E^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1,n}\hookrightarrow E^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have a right exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus W_{k-2}H^{k-2}(E^{n-2})\rightarrow W_{k}H^{k}(E^{n-1})\rightarrow W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus W_{k-2}H^{k-2}(E^{n-2})\rightarrow W_{k}H^{k}(E^{n-1})\rightarrow W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon E^{n-1}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon E^{n-1}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and proper, the Leray spectral sequence degenerates at <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Proposition 2.16]</cite>, we have a direct sum decomposition</span></p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(E^{n-1})=\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},R^{q}f_{*}\mathbb{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(E^{n-1})=\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},R^{q}f_{*}\mathbb{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">By the Künneth formula, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R^{q}f_{*}\mathbb{Q}=\bigoplus_{i_{2}+\cdots+i_{n}=q}R^{i_{2}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{n}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{q}f_{*}\mathbb{Q}=\bigoplus_{i_{2}+\cdots+i_{n}=q}R^{i_{2}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{n}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}:=R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}:=R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and note that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{0}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{0}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}=\mathbb{Q}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}=\mathbb{Q}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix some <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{2},\ldots,i_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{2},\ldots,i_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+i_{2}+\cdots+i_{n}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+i_{2}+\cdots+i_{n}=k</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, then we claim that</span></p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},R^{i_{2}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{n}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})\subset W_{k}H^{k}(E^{n-1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},R^{i_{2}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{n}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})\subset W_{k}H^{k}(E^{n-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">lies in the image of the first map in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>).
More precisely, let
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}\colon E^{n-2}\rightarrow E^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}\colon E^{n-2}\rightarrow E^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, be the locus where the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th entry in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the section <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\colon\mathcal{M}_{1,1}\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\colon\mathcal{M}_{1,1}\to E</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the fiber diagram</span></p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="70.11" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-34.92px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 114.6 70.11" width="114.6"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,70.11) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(56.31,0) translate(0,34.92)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -56.31 -25.9)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 69.84)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.33)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(19.85,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -13.89 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.01em;--ltx-fo-height:0.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.26)" width="27.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces E^{n-2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces E^{n-2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 39.7 0) translate(53.06,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -13.89 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.01em;--ltx-fo-height:0.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.26)" width="27.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces E^{n-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces E^{n-1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 60.82)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0.12 0) translate(19.73,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -13.77 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.41" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="27.54"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{M}_{1,1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{M}_{1,1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 46.17 0) translate(46.59,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -7.43 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.07em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="14.86"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces E.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces E.}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -36.46 13.25 L -36.46 -10.55"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -36.46 -10.82)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -16.33 22.05 L 15.77 22.05"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 16.05 22.05)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -6.18 28.04)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.89em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.9" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="12.36"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 36.46 13.25 L 36.46 -10.55"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 36.46 -10.82)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 39.71 0.35)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.13em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.9" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="15.6"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{pr}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">pr</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{pr}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -16.45 -22.44 L 22.24 -22.44"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 22.52 -22.44)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -0.03 -29.86)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.46em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.41"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}=2," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}=2,</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+i_{2}+\cdots+i_{j-1}+i_{j+1}+\cdots+i_{n}=k-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+i_{2}+\cdots+i_{j-1}+i_{j+1}+\cdots+i_{n}=k-2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, using the Leray spectral sequence for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{n-2}\to\mathcal{M}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{n-2}\to\mathcal{M}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a corresponding term</span></p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S2.E7.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:317.4pt;height:10.6pt;vertical-align:-3.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-19.1pt,0.6pt) scale(0.892438075857999,0.892438075857999) ;">
<p id="S2.E7.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.E7.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{aligned} W_{k-2}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},R^{i_{2}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{j-1}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes R^{i_{j+1}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{n}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})\subset W_{k-2}H^{k-2}(E^{n-2}).\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{aligned} W_{k-2}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},R^{i_{2}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{j-1}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes R^{i_{j+1}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{n}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})\subset W_{k-2}H^{k-2}(E^{n-2}).\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">Then the pushforward <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{j*}\colon H^{k-2}(E^{n-2})\to H^{k}(E^{n-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j*}\colon H^{k-2}(E^{n-2})\to H^{k}(E^{n-1})</annotation></semantics></math> sends
the subspace on the left of (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>) isomorphically onto the subspace on the left of (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>), which proves the claim.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the terms <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},R^{i_{2}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{n}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},R^{i_{2}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{n}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(E^{n-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(E^{n-1})</annotation></semantics></math> where all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, Section 2]</cite>, we have <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes q})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes q})=0</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=k-1</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes k-1})=\mathsf{S}_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes k-1})=\mathsf{S}_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> by Eichler–Shimura.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">There are <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n-1}{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n-1}{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> terms of the form <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes k-1})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>), coming from choosing which <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> indices have <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Each of these terms is pulled back along the projection map <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{n-1}\to E^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{n-1}\to E^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which remembers the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> factors for which <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be the collection of indices <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}=1</annotation></semantics></math> together with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a commutative diagram</span></p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="76.58" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-38.29px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 223 76.58" width="223"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,76.58) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(111.5,0) translate(0,38.29)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -111.5 -29.26)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 76.57)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.81)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(45.85,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -39.89 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.77em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.21" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="79.78"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W_{k}H^{k}(E^{n-1})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W_{k}H^{k}(E^{n-1})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 94.36 0) translate(79.6,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -40.43 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.84em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.71" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="80.86"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 67.55)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0.36 0) translate(45.49,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -39.53 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.71em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.21" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="79.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W_{k}H^{k}(E^{k-1})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W_{k}H^{k}(E^{k-1})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 91.7 0) translate(82.26,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -43.09 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.23em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.71" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="86.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,A}).}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,A}).}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -19.53 24.94 L 15.24 24.94"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.51 24.94)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -65.65 -12.18 L -65.65 12.12"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 -65.65 12.4)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -19.89 -25.81 L 12.57 -25.81"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.85 -25.81)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 62.45 -12.18 L 62.45 11.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 62.45 11.9)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the pullbacks from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,A})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> ranges over all subsets of size <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we note that there can be no relations among these <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n-1}{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n-1}{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes k-1})=\mathsf{S}_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{1,1},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes k-1})=\mathsf{S}_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> since the image of the left-hand map of (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>) lies in the subspace of type <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{i}\mathsf{S}_{k+1-2i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{i}\mathsf{S}_{k+1-2i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have now shown that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})=\mathsf{S}_{k+1}\otimes U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})=\mathsf{S}_{k+1}\otimes U</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n-1}{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n-1}{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector space <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. From the discussion above, it is not difficult to identify <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> as an <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-representation. When
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=k</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> action on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,k})</annotation></semantics></math> is the sign representation.
To identify <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n-1}\subset\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n-1}\subset\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subgroup that fixes <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is freely generated by the pullbacks from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{1,A})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> runs over subsets of size <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Res}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}_{\mathbb{S}_{n-1}}U=\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{k-1}\times\mathbb{S}_{n-k}}^{\mathbb{S}_{n-1}}(\mathrm{sgn}\boxtimes\mathbf{1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Res</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ind</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊠</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Res}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}_{\mathbb{S}_{n-1}}U=\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{k-1}\times\mathbb{S}_{n-k}}^{\mathbb{S}_{n-1}}(\mathrm{sgn}\boxtimes\mathbf{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">By the Pieri rule, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{k-1}\times\mathbb{S}_{n-k}}^{\mathbb{S}_{n-1}}(\mathrm{sgn}\boxtimes\mathbf{1})=\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{k-1}\times\mathbb{S}_{n-k}}^{\mathbb{S}_{n-1}}(V_{1^{k-1}}\boxtimes V_{n-k})=V_{n-k+1,1^{k-2}}\oplus V_{n-k,1^{k-1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ind</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊠</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ind</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊠</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{k-1}\times\mathbb{S}_{n-k}}^{\mathbb{S}_{n-1}}(\mathrm{sgn}\boxtimes\mathbf{1})=\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{k-1}\times\mathbb{S}_{n-k}}^{\mathbb{S}_{n-1}}(V_{1^{k-1}}\boxtimes V_{n-k})=V_{n-k+1,1^{k-2}}\oplus V_{n-k,1^{k-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">By the branching rule, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-k+1,1^{k-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-k+1,1^{k-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> representation whose restriction to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the representation in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{14}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>14</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{14}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> lies in the STE generated by <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the STE generated by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is the STE generated by the class <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> cusp form for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., it is the smallest STE whose complexification contains <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, we showed that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper, we show that it also contains <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>). In contrast with the system of tautological rings, an arbitrary STE need not be closed under pushforward along maps forgetting marked points or pullback to the boundary. Nevertheless, we have the following result for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\omega}^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\omega}^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The STE <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\omega}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\omega}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed under the tautological operations induced by pushforward for forgetting marked points and pullback for gluing marked points.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, all even cohomology in genus <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by boundary strata. Therefore, any product of two odd degree classes can be written as a sum of boundary strata. It follows that every class in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\omega}^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\omega}^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented as a linear combination of decorated graphs all of whose nontautological decorations are <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}</annotation></semantics></math>-classes on genus 1 vertices.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">The pushforward of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> along the maps forgetting marked points is zero. Since the tautological rings are closed under pushforward, it follows that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\omega}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\omega}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed under pushforward.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Lemma 2.2]</cite>, the image of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> under pullback to the boundary lies in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the excess intersection formula, and using the fact that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-classes in genus 1 are boundary classes, it follows that the pullback of any such decorated graph in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\omega}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\omega}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to any boundary stratum is a sum of decorated graphs of the same form. In particular, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\omega}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\omega}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed under pullback to the boundary, as required.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, the Hodge groups <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,k})</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the space of cusp forms for <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Essentially the same argument shows that the STE generated by any subset of these cusp form spaces is closed under all of the tautological operations.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.2. </span>Genus 2</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we summarize what we need about the pure weight cohomology of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2,n}</annotation></semantics></math> in low degrees, from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2k}H^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})=RH^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2k}H^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})=RH^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&lt;20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&lt;20</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=RH^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=RH^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, Theorem 3.8]</cite>. Hence, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2k}H^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})=RH^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2k}H^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})=RH^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> by restriction. For <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=20</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, the same result holds (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, Remark 3.10]</cite>).
For <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;20</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, all of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2k}H^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2k}H^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is pulled back from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2k}H^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2k}H^{2k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,m})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq 20</annotation></semantics></math> (see Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, below). Because the tautological ring is closed under forgetful pullbacks, the lemma follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Applying Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Preliminaries on STEs ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> to the STE <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> immediately implies Conjecture <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Conjecture 1.8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a> for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=RH^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})=RH^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In odd degrees, there are restrictions on the possible motivic structures coming from the cohomology of local systems on the moduli space of principally polarized abelian surfaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the category of Galois representations, the pure weight cohomology of <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2,n}</annotation></semantics></math> in degrees 13 and 15 is of the form</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{13}H^{13}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}\mbox{ \ \ and \ \ }W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{13}H^{13}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}\mbox{ \ \ and \ \ }W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For every <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the surjection</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\rightarrow W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\rightarrow W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">factors through <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n}^{\ct})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n}^{\ct})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2,n}^{\ct}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2,n}^{\ct}</annotation></semantics></math> is the moduli space of genus <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> curves of compact type with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> markings. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, Theorem 2.1(i) and (ii)]</cite>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n}^{\ct})\cong\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\ct},A^{q})\oplus H^{p}(\Sym^{2}\mathcal{M}_{1,1},B^{q})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n}^{\ct})\cong\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\ct},A^{q})\oplus H^{p}(\Sym^{2}\mathcal{M}_{1,1},B^{q}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> are local systems of weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, given by direct sums of Tate twists of symplectic local systems. Moreover, the terms <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p}(\Sym^{2}\mathcal{M}_{1,1},B^{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p}(\Sym^{2}\mathcal{M}_{1,1},B^{q})</annotation></semantics></math> map to zero under restriction to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, Lemma 3.3]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now consider the terms <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\ct},A^{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\ct},A^{q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Tate twist of a symplectic local system of weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\ct}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\ct}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, then <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\ct},\mathbb{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\ct},\mathbb{V})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes because the hyperelliptic involution acts on the fibers of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is even, the possible Galois representations appearing in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\ct},\mathbb{V})^{\mathrm{ss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{2}^{\ct},\mathbb{V})^{\mathrm{ss}}</annotation></semantics></math> are determined by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, Theorem 2.1]</cite>. When <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q=13</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q=15)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q=15)</annotation></semantics></math>, the only possibility of pure weight is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, in the category of Galois representations, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}\mbox{ \ \ and \ \ }H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}\mbox{ \ \ and \ \ }H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">For degree 13, consider the right exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{2,n}})\rightarrow H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\rightarrow W_{13}H^{13}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{2,n}})\rightarrow H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\rightarrow W_{13}H^{13}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, the semi-simplification of the left-hand side is a sum of terms <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>. Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 2.8. ‣ 2.2.2. Genus 2 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> shows that the semi-simplification of the right-hand side consists only of Tate twists of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the semi-simplification of the middle term does too.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly, for degree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the right exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{2,n}})\rightarrow H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\rightarrow W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{2,n}})\rightarrow H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})\rightarrow W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">The boundary divisors on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are finite quotients of products of moduli spaces for genus at most <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, by the Künneth formula and vanishing of odd cohomology in degrees less than or equal to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that the left-hand side is a sum of terms of the form <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}})\otimes H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}})\otimes H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime},g_{1},g_{2}\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime},g_{1},g_{2}\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. By the degree 13 result just proved, the semi-simplification of terms of the first kind is a sum of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>. Meanwhile, we know <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Tate by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}})^{\mathrm{ss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}})^{\mathrm{ss}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of terms <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Hence, the semi-simplification of terms of the second kind is also a sum of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>. To conclude, note that Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 2.8. ‣ 2.2.2. Genus 2 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> shows that the semi-simplification of the right-hand side consists only of Tate twists of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the semi-simplification of the middle term does too.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">See Lemma <a href="#S8.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 8.1. ‣ 8. Proof of Theorem ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a> for an analogous result in the category of Hodge structures.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Finite generation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be an STE. By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Preliminaries on STEs ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it surjects onto <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/\big(\Phi^{k}_{g,n}+RH^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/\big(\Phi^{k}_{g,n}+RH^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\big)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Below we give a sufficient criterion for the vanishing of <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/\Phi^{k}_{g,n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/\Phi^{k}_{g,n}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The argument is similar to the proof of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 2.2. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, using the Künneth decomposition of the Leray spectral sequence for the <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold fiber product of the universal curve <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We are grateful to Dan Petersen for explaining how an earlier version of this lemma could be strengthened to the version presented here.
This stronger version will also be useful for controlling some additional base cases in Section <a href="#S7" title="7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
To state it, we define the subspace</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{g,n}^{k}:=\psi_{1}\Phi^{k-2}_{g,n}+\cdots+\psi_{n}\Phi^{k-2}_{g,n}\subset W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{g,n}^{k}:=\psi_{1}\Phi^{k-2}_{g,n}+\cdots+\psi_{n}\Phi^{k-2}_{g,n}\subset W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In other words, <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{k}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{k}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by pullbacks from moduli spaces with fewer markings multiplied with <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-classes. Given
a <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple of integers <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=(\lambda_{1}\geq\lambda_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{g}\geq 0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=(\lambda_{1}\geq\lambda_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{g}\geq 0)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> be the associated symplectic local system on <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=\lambda_{1}+\cdots+\lambda_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=\lambda_{1}+\cdots+\lambda_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the weight of the local system <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=n</annotation></semantics></math>, as in the previous section, we write <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> for the irreducible <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> representation corresponding to <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a) If <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/(\Phi_{g,n}^{k}+\Psi_{g,n}^{k})\cong\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}W_{k}H^{k-n}(\mathcal{M}_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\lambda})\otimes V_{\lambda^{T}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/(\Phi_{g,n}^{k}+\Psi_{g,n}^{k})\cong\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}W_{k}H^{k-n}(\mathcal{M}_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\lambda})\otimes V_{\lambda^{T}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b) Moreover, if <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq k</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=\Phi^{k}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=\Phi^{k}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">(a) Let <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal curve.
There is an open inclusion <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\subset\mathcal{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\subset\mathcal{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence restriction gives a surjection from <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a subset <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{n}\to\mathcal{C}^{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>A</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{n}\to\mathcal{C}^{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection onto the factors indexed by <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider the following three subspaces of <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, the span of the pullbacks of <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{\{i\}^{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{\{i\}^{c}})</annotation></semantics></math> along projection <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{n}\to\mathcal{C}^{\{i\}^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{n}\to\mathcal{C}^{\{i\}^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Psi}</annotation></semantics></math>, the span of <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}\cdot H^{k-2}(\mathcal{C}^{\{i\}^{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}\cdot H^{k-2}(\mathcal{C}^{\{i\}^{c}})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, the span of <math id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{ij}\cdot H^{k-2}(\mathcal{C}^{\{i,j\}^{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{ij}\cdot H^{k-2}(\mathcal{C}^{\{i,j\}^{c}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the pullback of the class of the diagonal in <math id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\mathcal{C}^{\{i,j\}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\mathcal{C}^{\{i,j\}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note that <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the kernel of <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})\to H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})\to H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the images of the weight <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> parts of <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Psi}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})\to W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})\to W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> are the subspaces <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{k}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{k}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{k}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{k}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.
To prove part (a), it thus suffices to show that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})/(\widetilde{\Phi}+\widetilde{\Psi}+\widetilde{\Delta})\cong\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}H^{k-n}(\mathcal{M}_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\lambda})\otimes V_{\lambda^{T}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})/(\widetilde{\Phi}+\widetilde{\Psi}+\widetilde{\Delta})\cong\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}H^{k-n}(\mathcal{M}_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\lambda})\otimes V_{\lambda^{T}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">We explain how (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\mathcal{C}^{n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\mathcal{C}^{n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and proper, the Leray spectral sequence degenerates at <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Proposition 2.16]</cite>, we have a direct sum decomposition</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})=\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{g},R^{q}f_{*}\mathbb{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})=\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p}(\mathcal{M}_{g},R^{q}f_{*}\mathbb{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Applying the Künneth formula, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})=\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}p+q=k\\
i_{1}+\ldots+i_{n}=q\end{subarray}}H^{p}\left(\mathcal{M}_{g},R^{i_{1}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{n}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})=\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}p+q=k\\
i_{1}+\ldots+i_{n}=q\end{subarray}}H^{p}\left(\mathcal{M}_{g},R^{i_{1}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\otimes\cdots\otimes R^{i_{n}}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">As observed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, Section 5.2.2]</cite>, the subspace <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the span of the summands where some <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Meanwhile, modulo <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, the subspace <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Psi}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the span of summands where some <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. This follows from the formulas for the projector <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (which projects onto such summands) in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, Section 5.1]</cite>. Quotienting by <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}+\widetilde{\Psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}+\widetilde{\Psi}</annotation></semantics></math> thus leaves the term with <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}=\ldots=i_{n}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}=\ldots=i_{n}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The local system <math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th tensor power of the standard representation of <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Sp}_{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Sp}_{2g}</annotation></semantics></math>. It thus decomposes into a direct sum of irreducible local systems as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, Section 3.2]</cite>.
As explained there, we have a natural projection</span></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n}\to\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}\otimes V_{\lambda^{T}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n}\to\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}\otimes V_{\lambda^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">whose kernel is spanned by the image of the <math id="S3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> insertion maps</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n-2}\to(R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n-2}\to(R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.5.1" class="ltx_text">given by inserting the class of the symplectic form. Modulo other Künneth components, the class of the diagonal <math id="S3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the class of the symplectic form in <math id="S3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\mathcal{C})\otimes H^{1}(\mathcal{C})\subset H^{2}(\mathcal{C}^{\{i,j\}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\mathcal{C})\otimes H^{1}(\mathcal{C})\subset H^{2}(\mathcal{C}^{\{i,j\}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Summarizing, we have found</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})/(\widetilde{\Phi}+\widetilde{\Psi})\cong H^{k-n}(\mathcal{M}_{g},(R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n})\to\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}H^{k-n}(\mathbb{V}_{\lambda})\otimes V_{\lambda^{T}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\mathcal{C}^{n})/(\widetilde{\Phi}+\widetilde{\Psi})\cong H^{k-n}(\mathcal{M}_{g},(R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q})^{\otimes n})\to\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}H^{k-n}(\mathbb{V}_{\lambda})\otimes V_{\lambda^{T}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.6.1" class="ltx_text">and the kernel is spanned by <math id="S3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>. This establishes (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">(b) Now assume <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq k</annotation></semantics></math>. If some <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}+\cdots+i_{n}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}+\cdots+i_{n}=k</annotation></semantics></math>, we must have some other <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s^{\prime}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s^{\prime}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that
<math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Psi}\subset\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Psi}\subset\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{k}_{g,n}\subset\Phi^{k}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{k}_{g,n}\subset\Phi^{k}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus it suffices to see that the right-hand side of (a) vanishes when <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. This is immediate when <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>. Meanwhile, for the case <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=k</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathcal{M}_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\lambda})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathcal{M}_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\lambda})=0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is nontrivial.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We note a neat consequence of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>(a) in genus <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, which was also pointed out to us by Dan Petersen.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is odd. The STE generated by <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n-1}),H^{k-2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n-1}),H^{k-2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n-1})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k-2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k-2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,m})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq n+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq n+2</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The STE generated by <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n-1}),H^{k-2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n-1}),H^{k-2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n-1})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k-2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k-2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,m})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq n+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq n+2</annotation></semantics></math> contains all classes in <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> that are pushed forward from the boundary, pulled back from less marked points, or products of <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> classes with classes pulled back from less marked points. It therefore suffices to show that <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})/(\Phi^{k}_{2,n}+\Psi^{k}_{2,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n})/(\Phi^{k}_{2,n}+\Psi^{k}_{2,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>(a), this quotient is a sum of the pure cohomology of local systems of weight <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. But on <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the hyperelliptic involution acts on local systems of weight <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, so local systems of odd weight have no cohomology.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2307.08830v3/17orbital.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="299" height="219" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>The argument in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> shows that <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{17}H^{17}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/(\Phi^{17}_{g,n}+RH^{17}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>17</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>17</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>17</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>17</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{17}H^{17}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/(\Phi^{17}_{g,n}+RH^{17}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}))</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> outside the gray shaded region. Note that this quotient does <em id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> vanish for <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> equal to <math id="S3.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,17)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>17</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,17)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,14)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,14)</annotation></semantics></math>, which are pictured by purple dots.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the STE generated by the cohomology groups listed in the statement of the theorem.
By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Preliminaries on STEs ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, it suffices to check that <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> surjects onto <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k^{\prime}}H^{k^{\prime}}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})/\left(\Phi^{k^{\prime}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}+RH^{k^{\prime}}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k^{\prime}}H^{k^{\prime}}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})/\left(\Phi^{k^{\prime}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}+RH^{k^{\prime}}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{\prime},n^{\prime},k^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{\prime},n^{\prime},k^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}\leq k-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}\leq k-2</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>(b), the target vanishes when <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}&gt;k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}&gt;k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the vcd of <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it also vanishes when <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}&gt;4g^{\prime}-4+n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}&gt;4g^{\prime}-4+n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k^{\prime}}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k^{\prime}}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for
<math id="S3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}\leq\frac{2g^{\prime}-2}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}\leq\frac{2g^{\prime}-2}{3}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One can prove analogues of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Preliminaries on STEs ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> for homology. In particular, for each <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a finitely generated STE that contains <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, with explicit bounds on the generators.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>The Chow–Künneth generation Property</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we prove a key lemma about the cycle class map for spaces that have the following property.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth algebraic stack of finite type over a field, stratified by quotient stacks. We say that <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the <em id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Chow–Künneth generation Property</em> (CKgP) if for all algebraic stacks <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> (of finite type, stratified by quotient stacks) the exterior product</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}(X)\otimes A_{*}(Y)\rightarrow A_{*}(X\times Y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}(X)\otimes A_{*}(Y)\rightarrow A_{*}(X\times Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is surjective.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For convenience, we record here several properties of the CKgP, all of which are proven in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Section 3.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth algebraic stack of finite type over a field, stratified by quotient stacks.</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is open and </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is proper, surjective, representable by DM stacks, and </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> admits a finite stratification </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\coprod_{S\in\mathcal{S}}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></msub><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\coprod_{S\in\mathcal{S}}S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that each </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an affine bundle, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP if and only if </span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP, and </span><math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a Grassmann bundle, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S4.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\BGL_{n},\BSL_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>BGL</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>BSL</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\BGL_{n},\BSL_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, or </span><math id="S4.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BPGL_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BPGL</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BPGL_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and proper and has the CKgP, then the cycle class map for <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Lemma 3.11]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth but not necessarily proper and has the CKgP, the cycle class map is not necessarily an isomorphism. Nevertheless, we have the following useful substitute (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> for slightly different statements with similar proofs).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be an open substack of a smooth proper Deligne–Mumford stack <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> over the complex numbers. If <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP, then the cycle class map</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cl\colon\bigoplus_{i}A_{i}(X)\rightarrow\bigoplus_{k}W_{k}H^{k}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cl</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>k</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cl\colon\bigoplus_{i}A_{i}(X)\rightarrow\bigoplus_{k}W_{k}H^{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is surjective. In particular, if <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is odd then
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(X)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(X)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and if <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is even then <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Hodge–Tate.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:=\dim\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:=\dim\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:=\overline{X}\smallsetminus X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∖</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:=\overline{X}\smallsetminus X</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\subset\overline{X}\times\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\subset\overline{X}\times\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the diagonal.
Because <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP, the exterior product map</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{\ell=0}^{d}A^{\ell}(\overline{X})\otimes A^{d-\ell}(X)\rightarrow A^{d}(\overline{X}\times X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{\ell=0}^{d}A^{\ell}(\overline{X})\otimes A^{d-\ell}(X)\rightarrow A^{d}(\overline{X}\times X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">is surjective. We have the excision exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{d}(\overline{X}\times D)\rightarrow A^{d}(\overline{X}\times\overline{X})\rightarrow A^{d}(\overline{X}\times X)\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{d}(\overline{X}\times D)\rightarrow A^{d}(\overline{X}\times\overline{X})\rightarrow A^{d}(\overline{X}\times X)\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that we can write the class of the diagonal in <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{d}(\overline{X}\times\overline{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{d}(\overline{X}\times\overline{X})</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\Gamma+\Delta^{0}+\Delta^{1}+\cdots+\Delta^{d}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\Gamma+\Delta^{0}+\Delta^{1}+\cdots+\Delta^{d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}\times D</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear combination of cycles of the form <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}^{\ell}\times W_{i}^{d-\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}^{\ell}\times W_{i}^{d-\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}^{d-\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}^{d-\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> are subvarieties of <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of codimension <math id="S4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projections of <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}\times\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}\times\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to the first and second factors respectively. Given a class <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi\in H^{*}(\overline{X}\times\overline{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi\in H^{*}(\overline{X}\times\overline{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{*}\colon H^{*}(\overline{X})\to H^{*}(\overline{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{*}\colon H^{*}(\overline{X})\to H^{*}(\overline{X})</annotation></semantics></math> for the associated correspondence, defined by <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Psi_{*}\alpha=p_{2*}(p_{1}^{*}\alpha\cdot\Psi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{*}\alpha=p_{2*}(p_{1}^{*}\alpha\cdot\Psi)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in W_{k}H^{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in W_{k}H^{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> be a lift of <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(\overline{X})=H^{k}(\overline{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(\overline{X})=H^{k}(\overline{X})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\Delta_{*}\alpha=(\Gamma_{*}+\Delta^{0}_{*}+\Delta^{1}_{*}+\cdots+\Delta^{d}_{*})\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\Delta_{*}\alpha=(\Gamma_{*}+\Delta^{0}_{*}+\Delta^{1}_{*}+\cdots+\Delta^{d}_{*})\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">First, we study <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta^{\ell}_{*}\alpha=p_{2*}(p_{1}^{*}\alpha\cdot\Delta^{\ell})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\ell}_{*}\alpha=p_{2*}(p_{1}^{*}\alpha\cdot\Delta^{\ell}).</annotation></semantics></math>
Note that <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}^{*}\alpha\cdot\Delta^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}^{*}\alpha\cdot\Delta^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for dimension reasons if <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+2\ell&gt;2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+2\ell&gt;2d</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, since <math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is of relative dimension <math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, the pushforward by <math id="S4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of any cycle will vanish if <math id="S4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+2\ell&lt;2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+2\ell&lt;2d</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the only nonzero terms occur when <math id="S4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2d-2\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2d-2\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S4.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\ell}_{*}\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\ell}_{*}\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear combination of the form <math id="S4.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum c_{i}W_{i}^{d-\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum c_{i}W_{i}^{d-\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, so it lies in the image of the cycle class map.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, we study <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{*}\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{*}\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. It suffices to treat the case that <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the class of a subvariety of <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}\times\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}\times\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> contained in <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}\times D</annotation></semantics></math>, as it is a linear combination of such subvarieties. In this case, the map <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}|_{\Gamma}\colon\Gamma\rightarrow\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}|_{\Gamma}\colon\Gamma\rightarrow\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\rightarrow\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\rightarrow\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{*}\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{*}\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> maps to zero under the restriction to <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> because the correspondence map factors through the cohomology of the boundary <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha|_{X}=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha|_{X}=a</annotation></semantics></math> is in the image of the cycle class map.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Essentially the same argument (only with <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients) gives a similar statement for the cycle class map to <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic étale cohomology over an arbitrary field.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The first two authors have previously given many examples of moduli spaces <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> that have the CKgP and also satisfy <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite>. For the inductive arguments in this paper, we need more base cases in genus <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>. In the next section, we prove that <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{7,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{7,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{7,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{7,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. The table below records the previously known results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite> together with Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 1.10. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
