<p id="S5.SS2.p19.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p19.3.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 5.5. ‣ 5.2. The tetragonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>, <math id="S5.SS2.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo>′</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime*}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective. Moreover, by the projection formula and Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 5.5. ‣ 5.2. The tetragonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>, the image of <math id="S5.SS2.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\jmath" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ȷ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\jmath</annotation></semantics></math> is generated as an <math id="S5.SS2.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> module by the fundamental class <math id="S5.SS2.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Sigma_{3,n}]\in A^{*}(Y_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Sigma_{3,n}]\in A^{*}(Y_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, any class <math id="S5.SS2.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in A^{*}(Y_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in A^{*}(Y_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\psi^{*}\alpha_{0}+[\Sigma_{3,n}]\psi^{*}\alpha_{1}=\iota^{*}\phi^{*}\alpha_{0}+[\Sigma_{3,n}]\iota^{*}\phi^{*}\alpha_{1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ι</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\psi^{*}\alpha_{0}+[\Sigma_{3,n}]\psi^{*}\alpha_{1}=\iota^{*}\phi^{*}\alpha_{0}+[\Sigma_{3,n}]\iota^{*}\phi^{*}\alpha_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p19.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p19.4.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S5.SS2.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}\in A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}\in A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the projection formula,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{*}\alpha=[Y_{n}]\phi^{*}\alpha_{0}+[\Sigma_{3,n}]\phi^{*}\alpha_{1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{*}\alpha=[Y_{n}]\phi^{*}\alpha_{0}+[\Sigma_{3,n}]\phi^{*}\alpha_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p19.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p19.5.1" class="ltx_text">where now the fundamental class <math id="S5.SS2.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Sigma_{3,n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Sigma_{3,n}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a class on <math id="S5.SS2.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\Diamond}_{4,7,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\Diamond}_{4,7,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It thus suffices to show that the classes <math id="S5.SS2.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Y_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Y_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Sigma_{3,n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Sigma_{3,n}]</annotation></semantics></math> are in the image of <math id="S5.SS2.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Lemma 4.8]</cite>, <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\overline{\Sigma_{\ell}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\overline{\Sigma_{\ell}}]</annotation></semantics></math> is in the image of <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,7}^{\Diamond})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,7}^{\Diamond})</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Sigma_{3,n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Sigma_{3,n}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the pullback of <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Sigma_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Sigma_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,7}^{\Diamond})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,7,n}^{\Diamond})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,7}^{\Diamond})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,7,n}^{\Diamond})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Y_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Y_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the pullback of <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\overline{\Sigma_{2}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\overline{\Sigma_{2}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, both <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Sigma_{3,n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Sigma_{3,n}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Y_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Y_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> are in the image of <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,7,n}^{\Diamond})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,7,n}^{\Diamond})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, they are in the image of <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p21.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that proper, surjective maps induce surjective maps on rational Chow groups.
Since the map <math id="S5.SS2.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n}^{\prime}\colon\mathcal{H}_{4,7,n}^{\Diamond}\to\mathcal{M}_{7,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n}^{\prime}\colon\mathcal{H}_{4,7,n}^{\Diamond}\to\mathcal{M}_{7,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper with image <math id="S5.SS2.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{4}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{4}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, every class supported on the tetragonal locus is the pushforward of a class from <math id="S5.SS2.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,7,n}^{\Diamond}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,7,n}^{\Diamond}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Combining Lemmas <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1 (Lemma 10.5 of []). ‣ 5.2. The tetragonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 5.6. ‣ 5.2. The tetragonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a> therefore proves the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.7</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then all classes supported on <math id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{4}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{4}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>The pentagonal locus</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It remains to study the locus <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}=\mathcal{M}_{7,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}=\mathcal{M}_{7,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> of curves of gonality exactly <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>.
Mukai showed that every curve in <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> is realized as a linear section of the orthogonal Grassmannian in its spinor embedding <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,10)\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{15}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>15</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,10)\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{15}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.
To take advantage of this construction, we first develop a few lemmas about the orthogonal Grassmannian.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.1. </span>The orthogonal Grassmannian</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal rank <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> bundle on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BSO_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BSO_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>. The universal orthogonal Grassmannian is the quotient stack <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\OG(5,10)/\mathrm{SO}_{10}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\OG(5,10)/\mathrm{SO}_{10}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which we think of as the orthogonal Grassmann bundle with structure map <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon\OG(5,\mathcal{V})\to\BSO_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon\OG(5,\mathcal{V})\to\BSO_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, the pullback of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{U}\oplus\mathcal{U}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{U}\oplus\mathcal{U}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is the universal rank <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> subbundle on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.8</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The stack</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})\cong[\OG(5,10)/\mathrm{SO}_{10}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})\cong[\OG(5,10)/\mathrm{SO}_{10}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">has the CKgP. Moreover, its Chow ring is freely generated by the Chern classes of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\mathrm{span}\{e_{1},\ldots,e_{10}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\mathrm{span}\{e_{1},\ldots,e_{10}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector space with quadratic form</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=\left(\begin{matrix}0&amp;I_{5}\\
I_{5}&amp;0\end{matrix}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=\left(\begin{matrix}0&amp;I_{5}\\
I_{5}&amp;0\end{matrix}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\mathrm{span}\{e_{1},\ldots,e_{5}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\mathrm{span}\{e_{1},\ldots,e_{5}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is an isotropic subspace. The stabilizer of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,10)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Stab}_{U}=\left\{M=\left(\begin{matrix}A&amp;B\\
0&amp;D\end{matrix}\right):M^{T}QM=Q\right\}\subset\mathrm{SO}_{10}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>D</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.278em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Stab}_{U}=\left\{M=\left(\begin{matrix}A&amp;B\\
0&amp;D\end{matrix}\right):M^{T}QM=Q\right\}\subset\mathrm{SO}_{10}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Expanding, we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{T}QM=\left(\begin{matrix}A^{T}&amp;0\\
B^{T}&amp;D^{T}\end{matrix}\right)\left(\begin{matrix}0&amp;I_{5}\\
I_{5}&amp;0\end{matrix}\right)\left(\begin{matrix}A&amp;B\\
0&amp;D\end{matrix}\right)=\left(\begin{matrix}0&amp;A^{T}D\\
D^{T}A&amp;B^{T}D+D^{T}B\end{matrix}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>D</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{T}QM=\left(\begin{matrix}A^{T}&amp;0\\
B^{T}&amp;D^{T}\end{matrix}\right)\left(\begin{matrix}0&amp;I_{5}\\
I_{5}&amp;0\end{matrix}\right)\left(\begin{matrix}A&amp;B\\
0&amp;D\end{matrix}\right)=\left(\begin{matrix}0&amp;A^{T}D\\
D^{T}A&amp;B^{T}D+D^{T}B\end{matrix}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Stab}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Stab}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the conditions <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=(A^{T})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=(A^{T})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{T}D+D^{T}B=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{T}D+D^{T}B=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note that <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Stab}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Stab}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximal parabolic subgroup and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,10)=\mathrm{SO}_{10}/\mathrm{Stab}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,10)=\mathrm{SO}_{10}/\mathrm{Stab}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As such, the quotient <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\OG(5,10)/\mathrm{SO}_{10}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\OG(5,10)/\mathrm{SO}_{10}]</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the classifying stack <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BStab}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BStab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BStab}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. To gain a better understanding of the latter, consider
the group homomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{5}\hookrightarrow\mathrm{Stab}_{U},\qquad A\mapsto\left(\begin{matrix}A&amp;0\\
0&amp;(A^{T})^{-1}\end{matrix}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{5}\hookrightarrow\mathrm{Stab}_{U},\qquad A\mapsto\left(\begin{matrix}A&amp;0\\
0&amp;(A^{T})^{-1}\end{matrix}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">For fixed <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, the condition <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{T}D+D^{T}B=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{T}D+D^{T}B=0</annotation></semantics></math> is linear in <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, it says that, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{T})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{T})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> translation of the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{10}</annotation></semantics></math> of skew symmetric <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 5</annotation></semantics></math> matrices.
In particular, the cosets of the subgroup <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{5}\hookrightarrow\mathrm{Stab}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{5}\hookrightarrow\mathrm{Stab}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic to affine spaces <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, the induced map on classifying spaces <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BGL_{5}\to\mathrm{BStab}_{U}\cong\OG(5,\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>BGL</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>BStab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BGL_{5}\to\mathrm{BStab}_{U}\cong\OG(5,\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math> is an affine bundle.
It follows that
<math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP by
Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>(<a href="#S4.I1.i4" title="item 4 ‣ Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.I1.i6" title="item 6 ‣ Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore,
by construction, the tautological subbundle <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math> pulls back to the tautological rank <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> bundle on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BGL_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BGL</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BGL_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows that
<math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathrm{BStab}_{U})\cong A^{*}(\mathrm{BGL}_{5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>BStab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>BGL</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathrm{BStab}_{U})\cong A^{*}(\mathrm{BGL}_{5})</annotation></semantics></math>, and is freely generated by the Chern classes of the tautological bundle.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.9</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is also a natural map <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Stab}_{U}\to\GL_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>GL</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Stab}_{U}\to\GL_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> that sends <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\begin{matrix}A&amp;B\\
0&amp;D\end{matrix}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>D</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\begin{matrix}A&amp;B\\
0&amp;D\end{matrix}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. The kernel of <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Stab}_{U}\to\GL_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>GL</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Stab}_{U}\to\GL_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subgroup <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\cong(\mathbb{G}_{a})^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\cong(\mathbb{G}_{a})^{10}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=I_{5},D=I_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=I_{5},D=I_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B+B^{T}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B+B^{T}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows that <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Stab}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Stab}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is actually a semi-direct product <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\rtimes\GL_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋊</mo><msub><mi>GL</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\rtimes\GL_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>. The map <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BStab}_{U}\to\BGL_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>BStab</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>BGL</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BStab}_{U}\to\BGL_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{B}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{B}G</annotation></semantics></math>-banded gerbe.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.10</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a rank <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\nu</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle with quadratic form on <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The rational Chow ring of </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(\nu,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(\nu,V)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is generated over the Chow ring of </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> by the Chern classes of the tautological subbundle.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP, then </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(\nu,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(\nu,V)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The argument is very similar to that for Grassmannians in type A.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">First consider the case when <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a point. The orthogonal Grassmannian <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(\nu,2\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(\nu,2\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> is stratified by Schubert cells, each of which is isomorphic to an affine space. By Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>(<a href="#S4.I1.i3" title="item 3 ‣ Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), it follows that <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(\nu,2\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(\nu,2\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP.
Moreover, the fundamental classes of these cells are expressed in terms of the Chern classes of the tautological quotient or subbundle via a Giambelli formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, p. 1–2]</cite>. Note that this formula involves dividing by <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, so it is important that we work with rational coefficients for this claim. In conclusion, the Chern classes of the tautological subbundle generate the Chow ring of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(\nu,2\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(\nu,2\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">More generally for a fiber bundle <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\OG(\nu,V)\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\OG(\nu,V)\to X</annotation></semantics></math>, to prove (1), we stratify <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> into locally closed subsets <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> over which <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, such that <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}_{i}\supset X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊃</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}_{i}\supset X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension at least <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. To check that the desired classes generate <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{k}(\OG(\nu,V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{k}(\OG(\nu,V))</annotation></semantics></math> for a given <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to show that they generate <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{k}(f^{-1}(X_{0}\cup X_{1}\cup\cdots\cup X_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{k}(f^{-1}(X_{0}\cup X_{1}\cup\cdots\cup X_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Over each piece of the stratification, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(X_{i})=X_{i}\times\OG(\nu,2\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(X_{i})=X_{i}\times\OG(\nu,2\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(\nu,2\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(\nu,2\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP, the Chow ring of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(X_{i})=X_{i}\times\OG(\nu,2\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(X_{i})=X_{i}\times\OG(\nu,2\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and restrictions of the Chern classes from the tautological subbundle on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(\nu,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(\nu,V)</annotation></semantics></math>. By excision and the push-pull formula, the Chow ring of any finite union <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(X_{0})\cup f^{-1}(X_{1})\cup\cdots\cup f^{-1}(X_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(X_{0})\cup f^{-1}(X_{1})\cup\cdots\cup f^{-1}(X_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the desired classes.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally (2) follows from (1) exactly as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Lemma 3.7]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.11</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold fiber product</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}:=\OG(5,\mathcal{V})\times_{\BSO_{10}}\cdots\times_{\BSO_{10}}\OG(5,\mathcal{V})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mi>OG</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>×</mo><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></msub><mi>OG</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}:=\OG(5,\mathcal{V})\times_{\BSO_{10}}\cdots\times_{\BSO_{10}}\OG(5,\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">has the CKgP and its Chow ring is generated by the Chern classes of the tautological subbundles <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{1},\ldots,\mathcal{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{1},\ldots,\mathcal{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> pulled back from each factor.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">The case <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> follows from Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 5.8. ‣ 5.3.1. The orthogonal Grassmannian ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>. For <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold fiber product is an orthogonal Grassmann bundle over the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-fold fiber product, so the claim follows from Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 5.10. ‣ 5.3.1. The orthogonal Grassmannian ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.12</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>(<a href="#S4.I1.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), the fact that <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP implies that <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BSO_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BSO_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> also has the CKgP.
It should be possible to show that <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BSO_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BSO_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP (with integral coefficients as well) using the calculation of its Chow ring by Field <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. If the calculation there holds over any field, it would show that <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BSO_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BSO_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> has Totaro’s “weak Chow–Künneth Property,” which is equivalent to the CKgP by the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.2. </span>Review of the Mukai construction</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first review Mukai’s construction and then explain how to modify it for pointed curves.
The canonical model of a pentagonal genus <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> curve <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> lies on a <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space of quadrics.
The vector space <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> of these quadrics is defined by the exact sequence</p>
<table id="S5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow W\rightarrow\Sym^{2}H^{0}(C,\omega_{C})\to H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}^{\otimes 2})\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow W\rightarrow\Sym^{2}H^{0}(C,\omega_{C})\to H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}^{\otimes 2})\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, the subspace <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{p}\subset W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{p}\subset W</annotation></semantics></math> of quadrics that are singular at <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional. It appears in the exact sequence (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Section 3]</cite>)</p>
<table id="S5.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow W_{p}\rightarrow\Sym^{2}H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}(-p))\to H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}(-p)^{\otimes 2})\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow W_{p}\rightarrow\Sym^{2}H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}(-p))\to H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}(-p)^{\otimes 2})\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Mukai shows that <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> has a canonical quadratic form and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{p}^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{p}^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isotropic subspace, so one obtains a map <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\OG(5,W^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\OG(5,W^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\mapsto[W_{p}^{\perp}]\in\OG(5,W^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\mapsto[W_{p}^{\perp}]\in\OG(5,W^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Theorem 0.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,10)\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}S^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,10)\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}S^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> be the spinor embedding, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Section 1]</cite>.
The composition</p>
<table id="S5.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\OG(5,W^{\vee})\cong\OG(5,10)\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}S^{+}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\OG(5,W^{\vee})\cong\OG(5,10)\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}S^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">realizes <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> as a linear section <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\OG(5,10)\cap\mathbb{P}^{6}\subset\mathbb{P}S^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\OG(5,10)\cap\mathbb{P}^{6}\subset\mathbb{P}S^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> is canonically embedded <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Theorem 0.4]</cite>. Conversely, if a linear section <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{6}\cap\OG(5,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{6}\cap\OG(5,10)</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth curve, then it is a canonically embedded pentagonal genus <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> curve <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Proposition 2.2]</cite>.
This construction works in families, and Mukai proves that there is an equivalence of stacks</p>
<table id="S5.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}\cong[(\Gr(7,S^{+})\smallsetminus\Delta)/\mathrm{SO}_{10}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}\cong[(\Gr(7,S^{+})\smallsetminus\Delta)/\mathrm{SO}_{10}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\subset\Gr(7,S^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\subset\Gr(7,S^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> is the closed locus of linear subspaces <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{6}\subset\mathbb{P}S^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{6}\subset\mathbb{P}S^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> whose intersection with <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,10)</annotation></semantics></math> is not a smooth curve <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Section 5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The
spinor representation of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a rank <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> on the classifying stack <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BSO_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BSO_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the equivalence (<a href="#S5.E10" title="In 5.3.2. Review of the Mukai construction ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>) shows that <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open substack of the Grassmann bundle <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BSO_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BSO_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.E11.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="71.17" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-35.58px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 159.66 71.17" width="159.66"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,71.17) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(79.83,0) translate(0,35.59)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -79.83 -28.45)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 71.17)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.08)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(22.4,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -16.44 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.38em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.6" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="19.05"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.E11.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 44.8 0) translate(74.04,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -34.87 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.04em;--ltx-fo-height:0.8em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.48" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.02)" width="55.9"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.E11.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 64.03)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 49.26 0) translate(69.57,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -30.41 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="46.98"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.E11.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\BSO_{10}.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\BSO_{10}.}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -32.76 22.96 L -2.66 22.96"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -32.76 22.96)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 2.71 C 0.95 2.71 1.72 2.1 1.72 1.36 C 1.72 0.61 0.95 0 0 0"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -2.38 22.96)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -24.73 27.6)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.55" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="12.59"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.E11.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 39 10.7 L 39 -13.1"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 39 -13.37)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We note that the tautological subbundle <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to the dual of the Hodge bundle on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, if <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> is the universal curve, then <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}^{*}\mathcal{E}=(f_{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}^{*}\mathcal{E}=(f_{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The universal version of the Mukai construction furnishes a map <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}\to\OG(5,\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\to\OG(5,\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BSO_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BSO_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.3. </span>The Mukai construction with markings</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, we describe <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> in a similar fashion to (<a href="#S5.E11" title="In 5.3.2. Review of the Mukai construction ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>), but this time as an open substack of a Grassmann bundle over <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To do so, let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota\colon\OG(5,\mathcal{V})\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota\colon\OG(5,\mathcal{V})\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal spinor embedding and let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}:=\iota^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+}}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}:=\iota^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+}}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The embedding <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by an inclusion of vector bundles <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\hookrightarrow\pi^{*}\mathcal{S}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\hookrightarrow\pi^{*}\mathcal{S}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold fiber product <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the pullbacks of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, from the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th factor. Let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{n}\colon\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}\to\BSO_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{n}\colon\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}\to\BSO_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> be the structure map.
Consider the sum of the inclusions</p>
<table id="S5.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{L}_{i}\xrightarrow{\phi_{n}}\pi_{n}^{*}\mathcal{S}^{+}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">n</mi></msub></mover><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{L}_{i}\xrightarrow{\phi_{n}}\pi_{n}^{*}\mathcal{S}^{+}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{n}\subset\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{n}\subset\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the open substack where <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the locus where the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math> have independent image under the spinor embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the cokernel of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{n}|_{Z_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{n}|_{Z_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a rank <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16-n</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The fiber of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{1},\ldots,p_{n})\in\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{1},\ldots,p_{n})\in\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizes linear spaces <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{6}\subset\mathbb{P}S^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{6}\subset\mathbb{P}S^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> that contain the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we can identify <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> with the locally closed substack</p>
<table id="S5.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{(p_{1},\ldots,p_{n},\Lambda):p_{i}\in\mathbb{P}\Lambda\text{ and $p_{i}$ independent}\right\}\subset\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}\times_{\BSO_{10}}\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> and </mtext><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> independent</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{(p_{1},\ldots,p_{n},\Lambda):p_{i}\in\mathbb{P}\Lambda\text{ and $p_{i}$ independent}\right\}\subset\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}\times_{\BSO_{10}}\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.13</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an open embedding <math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Grassmann bundle</span></p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="73.89" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-36.94px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 273.99 73.89" width="273.99"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,73.89) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(137,0) translate(0,36.95)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -137 -28.42)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 73.89)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.44)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(20.39,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -14.43 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.09em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.49" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="28.86"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 40.78 0) translate(78.1,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -38.2 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.52em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="76.39"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 65.37)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 69.99 0) translate(48.89,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -8.98 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.3em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="17.97"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 163.03 0) translate(72.45,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -32.55 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.7em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="65.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}.}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -93.95 24.96 L -63.1 24.96"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -93.95 24.96)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 2.71 C 0.95 2.71 1.72 2.1 1.72 1.36 C 1.72 0.61 0.95 0 0 0"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -62.82 24.96)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -86.23 29.6)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.01em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.55" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="13.98"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -18.12 12.7 L -18.12 -13.07"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -18.12 -13.35)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -0.91 -24.96 L 59.15 -24.96"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -0.91 -24.96)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 2.71 C 0.95 2.71 1.72 2.1 1.72 1.36 C 1.72 0.61 0.95 0 0 0"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 59.43 -24.96)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hence, <math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal curve and let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\colon\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\to\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\colon\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\to\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th section.
The universal version of the Mukai construction gives a morphism <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}\to\OG(5,\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\to\OG(5,\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math>. Precomposing with each of the sections <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a map <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\to\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\to\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BSO_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BSO_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that, for <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, the images of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> must be independent. Indeed, suppose the images of the sections in a fiber, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, are dependent under the canonical embedding. Then <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}+\cdots+p_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}+\cdots+p_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> would give a <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, but curves in <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> have no <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math> by definition.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We also have the map <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\to\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}\to\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\to\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}\to\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> that sends a curve to its span under the spinor embedding.
Taking the product of these maps over <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BSO_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BSO_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a map</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\to\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}\times_{\BSO_{10}}\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>BSO</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\to\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}\times_{\BSO_{10}}\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">This map sends a family of pointed curves <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,p_{1},\ldots,p_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,p_{1},\ldots,p_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> over a scheme <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to the data of sections <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}\colon T\to C\to\OG(5,W^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}\colon T\to C\to\OG(5,W^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math> and the subbundle of the spinor representation of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> determined by the span of the fibers of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\OG(5,W^{\vee})\subset\mathbb{P}S^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\OG(5,W^{\vee})\subset\mathbb{P}S^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. The map evidently factors through the locally closed locus in (<a href="#S5.E13" title="In 5.3.3. The Mukai construction with markings ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>), which we identified with <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, the image is precisely <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})\smallsetminus\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})\smallsetminus\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the closed locus such that <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\Lambda\cap\OG(5,W^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\Lambda\cap\OG(5,W^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math> is not a family of smooth curves. Indeed, on the complement of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, an inverse map <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})\smallsetminus\Delta\to\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})\smallsetminus\Delta\to\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined
by sending <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{1},\ldots,p_{n},\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{1},\ldots,p_{n},\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> to the curve <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\mathbb{P}\Lambda\cap\OG(5,W^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\mathbb{P}\Lambda\cap\OG(5,W^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math> together with the sections <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}\in\mathbb{P}\Lambda\cap\OG(5,W^{\vee})=C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}\in\mathbb{P}\Lambda\cap\OG(5,W^{\vee})=C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm11" title="Corollary 5.11. ‣ 5.3.1. The orthogonal Grassmannian ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>, we know <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP. To complete the proof, apply Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>(<a href="#S4.I1.i1" title="item 1 ‣ Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.I1.i5" title="item 5 ‣ Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now identify the restrictions of the universal bundles on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.14</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal curve and let <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the image of the <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th section.
The vector bundle <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{n}^{*}\,\mathcal{U}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{n}^{*}\,\mathcal{U}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> sits in an exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S5.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\alpha_{n}^{*}\,\mathcal{U}_{i}\rightarrow\Sym^{2}f_{*}(\omega_{f}(-\sigma_{i}))\rightarrow f_{*}((\omega_{f}(-\sigma_{i}))^{\otimes 2})\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\alpha_{n}^{*}\,\mathcal{U}_{i}\rightarrow\Sym^{2}f_{*}(\omega_{f}(-\sigma_{i}))\rightarrow f_{*}((\omega_{f}(-\sigma_{i}))^{\otimes 2})\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, all classes pulled back from <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The composition of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with projection onto the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th factor of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the map that sends a pointed curve <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,p_{1},\ldots,p_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,p_{1},\ldots,p_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> to the image of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> under the canonical map from <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,W^{\vee})\cong\OG(5,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,W^{\vee})\cong\OG(5,10)</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, the fiber at <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the universal rank <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> bundle on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,10)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{p}^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{p}^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>. Equation (<a href="#S5.E14" title="In Lemma 5.14. ‣ 5.3.3. The Mukai construction with markings ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a>) is the relative version of (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In 5.3.2. Review of the Mukai construction ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a>). By Grothendieck–Riemann–Roch, the middle and right terms in (<a href="#S5.E14" title="In Lemma 5.14. ‣ 5.3.3. The Mukai construction with markings ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a>) have tautological Chern classes.
It follows that the Chern classes of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{U}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{U}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are also tautological. The last claim now follows from Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm11" title="Corollary 5.11. ‣ 5.3.1. The orthogonal Grassmannian ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.15</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{L}_{i})=-\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{L}_{i})=-\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal curve. The line bundle <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pullback of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+}}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+}}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math> along the composition</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\xrightarrow{\sigma_{i}}\mathcal{C}\rightarrow\OG(5,\mathcal{V})\xrightarrow{\iota}\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜄</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\xrightarrow{\sigma_{i}}\mathcal{C}\rightarrow\OG(5,\mathcal{V})\xrightarrow{\iota}\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">But the above composition also factors as</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\xrightarrow{\sigma_{i}}\mathcal{C}\xrightarrow{|\omega_{f}|}\mathbb{P}(f_{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}\to\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ω</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">f</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mover><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\xrightarrow{\sigma_{i}}\mathcal{C}\xrightarrow{|\omega_{f}|}\mathbb{P}(f_{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}\to\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+}}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{S}^{+}}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}(f_{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}(f_{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}(f_{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}}(-1)=(\sigma_{i}^{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}(f_{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}}(-1)=(\sigma_{i}^{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.16</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the tautological rank <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7-n</annotation></semantics></math> subbundle on <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The pullback <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{E}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{E}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> sits in an exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S5.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{L}_{i}\rightarrow(f_{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}\rightarrow\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{E}_{n}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{L}_{i}\rightarrow(f_{*}\omega_{f})^{\vee}\rightarrow\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{E}_{n}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, the Chern classes of <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{E}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{n}^{*}\mathcal{E}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that we defined <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as the cokernel of (<a href="#S5.E12" title="In 5.3.3. The Mukai construction with markings ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a>).
The map onto the second factor in (<a href="#S5.E13" title="In 5.3.3. The Mukai construction with markings ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>), <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})\to\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})\to\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>, sends a <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7-n</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional subspace to its preimage under the quotient map <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{+}\to\mathcal{Q}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{+}\to\mathcal{Q}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, there is an exact sequence on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{L}_{i}\to p^{*}\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{E}_{n}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{L}_{i}\to p^{*}\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{E}_{n}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then note that <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{n}^{*}p^{*}\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{n}^{*}p^{*}\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as the pullback of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\to\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}\to\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}\to\mathcal{M}_{7}^{\circ}\to\Gr(7,\mathcal{S}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the dual of the Hodge bundle. The last claim follows by combining the exact sequence (<a href="#S5.E15" title="In Lemma 5.16. ‣ 5.3.3. The Mukai construction with markings ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.15</span></a>) with Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm15" title="Lemma 5.15. ‣ 5.3.3. The Mukai construction with markings ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.15</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.17</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Chow ring of <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by tautological classes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm13" title="Lemma 5.13. ‣ 5.3.3. The Mukai construction with markings ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>, we know that <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open substack of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr(7-n,\mathcal{Q}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. The Chow ring of the latter is generated by pullbacks of classes from <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\OG(5,\mathcal{V})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and by the Chern classes of the tautological subbundle <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemmas <a href="#S5.Thmthm14" title="Lemma 5.14. ‣ 5.3.3. The Mukai construction with markings ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmthm16" title="Lemma 5.16. ‣ 5.3.3. The Mukai construction with markings ‣ 5.3. The pentagonal locus ‣ 5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a> respectively, both of these collections of classes restrict to tautological classes on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7,n}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Applications to even cohomology</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we use the results from Sections <a href="#S3" title="3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#S4" title="4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, and <a href="#S5" title="5. The CKgP in genus 7 with at most 3 marked points ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>The degree 4 cohomology of <math id="S6.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>(1).</h6>
<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, we know that <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, using Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, we deduce that <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is contained the STE generated by <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}&lt;7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}&lt;7</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{4}H^{4}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{4}H^{4}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
in this range, and it follows that <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>(2).</h6>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>(1) and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, all classes in <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> that are pushed forward from the boundary are tautological. Also, <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is stable and hence tautological for <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. It follows that <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, to show that <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for all <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, it would suffice to show this for <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math>. In principle, this can be checked computationally as follows. By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>(<a href="#S1.I1.i3" title="item 3 ‣ Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we know that <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for all <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, if the intersection pairing</p>
<table id="S6.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\times RH_{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow\mathbb{Q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\times RH_{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is perfect for <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{6}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological. In principle, one could compute this pairing using the Sage package admcycles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. In practice, however, this computation is too memory intensive to carry out.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>The low degree even homology of <math id="S6.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}^{BM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}^{BM}</annotation></semantics></math> denote Borel–Moore homology with coefficients in <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with its mixed Hodge structure or Galois action.
From the long exact sequence in Borel–Moore homology, we have a right exact sequence</p>
<table id="S6.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})\rightarrow H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow W_{-k}H_{k}^{BM}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})\rightarrow H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow W_{-k}H_{k}^{BM}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>(3).</h6>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now show that <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for even <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 14</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. For <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Proposition 2.1]</cite> and the duality between <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}^{BM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}^{BM}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}^{BM}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0\text{ for }\begin{cases}k&lt;2g\text{ and }n=0,1;\\
k&lt;2g-2+n\text{ and }n\geq 2.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}^{BM}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0\text{ for }\begin{cases}k&lt;2g\text{ and }n=0,1;\\
k&lt;2g-2+n\text{ and }n\geq 2.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Combining this with the exact sequence (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6.2. The low degree even homology of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         is tautological ‣ 6. Applications to even cohomology ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>), we reduce inductively to the finitely many cases <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2g-2+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2g-2+n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, in all of these cases with <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 14</annotation></semantics></math> we know that <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> (see Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). By Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, it follows that <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-k}H^{BM}_{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-k}H^{BM}_{k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological, so again by induction and the exact sequence (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6.2. The low degree even homology of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         is tautological ‣ 6. Applications to even cohomology ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>) we reduce to the cases of <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>, all even cohomology is tautological <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. When <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Corollary 2.7. ‣ 2.2.2. Genus 2 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As Hodge structures or Galois representations, we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2d_{g,n}-14}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-7}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2d_{g,n}-14}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-7}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We proved <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{14}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>14</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{14}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological and hence algebraic. Applying Poincaré duality yields the corollary.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of <math id="S7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a>. As a corollary, we obtain restrictions on the Hodge structures and Galois representations appearing in <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>Thirteenth homology</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As usual, we argue by induction on <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. First, we must treat one extra base case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The group <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,11})</annotation></semantics></math> is in the STE generated by <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We showed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> that <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is in the STE generated by <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next, we note that <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2,10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2,10}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP (see Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), so <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,10})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of classes pushed forward from the boundary by Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>. In particular, <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,10})</annotation></semantics></math> also lies in this STE.
The claim then follows from Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is similar to that of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>(3).
When <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, all odd cohomology vanishes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, so we can assume <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Proposition 2.1]</cite> and the duality between <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{13}^{BM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{13}^{BM}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>13</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that for <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{BM}_{13}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0\text{ for }\begin{cases}13&lt;2g\text{ and }n=0,1\\
13&lt;2g-2+n\text{ and }n\geq 2.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{BM}_{13}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0\text{ for }\begin{cases}13&lt;2g\text{ and }n=0,1\\
13&lt;2g-2+n\text{ and }n\geq 2.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Combining this with the exact sequence (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6.2. The low degree even homology of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         is tautological ‣ 6. Applications to even cohomology ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>), we reduce inductively to the finitely many cases where <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13\geq 2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13\geq 2g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13\geq 2g-2+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13\geq 2g-2+n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. All such cases except for <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\{(2,11),(1,11),(1,12),(1,13)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\{(2,11),(1,11),(1,12),(1,13)\}</annotation></semantics></math> have the CKgP (see Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). By Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, it follows that <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-13}H_{13}^{BM}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-13}H_{13}^{BM}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)\not\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)\not\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>. By induction and the exact sequence (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6.2. The low degree even homology of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         is tautological ‣ 6. Applications to even cohomology ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>), we reduce to the exceptional cases of <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>. The case <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)=(2,11)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)=(2,11)</annotation></semantics></math> follows from Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 7.1. ‣ 7.1. Thirteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> and Hard Lefschetz. The cases where <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> follow from Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 2.2. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> and induction on <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Following the proof strategy above, we obtain the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 7.2</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As Hodge structures or Galois representations, we have</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2d_{g,n}-13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-12}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2d_{g,n}-13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-12}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">From the proof above, we see that <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-13}H_{13}^{BM}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-13}H_{13}^{BM}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)\notin Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)\notin Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and so</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{13}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})\rightarrow H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{13}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})\rightarrow H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">is surjective. Noting that pushforward along a gluing map just induces a Tate twist, by induction on <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we reduce to the cases <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>. The case <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)=(2,11)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)=(2,11)</annotation></semantics></math> follows from Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 7.1. ‣ 7.1. Thirteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> and Hard Lefschetz. The cases where <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> follow from Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 2.2. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> and induction on <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2. </span>Fifteenth homology</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Again, we will argue by induction on <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. We first need to treat two additional base cases in genus <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. The difficult one is with <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> markings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is generated by classes pushed forward from the boundary. Hence, <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the STE generated by <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 7.3. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a> involves calculating intersection pairings via decorated graphs. In order to simplify pictures for and language surrounding decorated graphs, we use the following conventions:</p>
<ol id="S7.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A white circle <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math> is a vertex of genus <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A black circle <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> is a vertex of genus <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All markings that are not drawn elsewhere are understood to be on the leftmost genus <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> vertex</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The decoration <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on a genus <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> vertex is shorthand for <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}^{*}\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}^{*}\omega</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math> forgets the <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th marking and <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is shorthand for <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{ij}^{*}\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{ij}^{*}\omega</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{ij}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,13}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{ij}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,13}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math> forgets the <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th and <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th markings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As we vary the decoration <math id="S7.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S7.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\cong\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\cong\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>, pushforward along a map that glues <math id="S7.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> pairs of marked points determines a morphism <math id="S7.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12}\to H^{11+2e}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12}\to H^{11+2e}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that a collection of <math id="S7.I1.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated graphs “is a basis for <math id="S7.I1.i5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>” if the corresponding morphisms <math id="S7.I1.i5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12}\to H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12}\to H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> form a basis for <math id="S7.I1.i5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom(\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12},H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom(\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12},H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}))</annotation></semantics></math> and we have <math id="S7.I1.i5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\cong\Hom(\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12},H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}))\otimes\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>e</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\cong\Hom(\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12},H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}))\otimes\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Bergström and Faber’s implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> of the Getzler–Kapranov formula in genus <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> shows that, viewed as Galois representations,</p>
<table id="S7.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})^{\mathrm{ss}}=836\,\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>836</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})^{\mathrm{ss}}=836\,\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In fact, we will produce <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="836" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>836</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">836</annotation></semantics></math> independent maps <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}\to H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}\to H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})</annotation></semantics></math> that arise through pushforward from the boundary. From this, we will conclude that <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})</annotation></semantics></math> is semi-simple and decomposes as a sum of these <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="836" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>836</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">836</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed above, an <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated graph with <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> edges represents a morphism <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12}\to H^{11+2e}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{e}\mathsf{S}_{12}\to H^{11+2e}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In Figure <a href="#S7.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Proof of Lemma . ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we list <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="891" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>891</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">891</annotation></semantics></math> such decorated graphs for <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})</annotation></semantics></math> and record particular Künneth components of their pullbacks to boundary divisors. We then argue that there are <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>55</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">55</annotation></semantics></math> relations among these graphs, which allows us to conclude that there are <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="836" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>836</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">836</annotation></semantics></math> independent graphs among them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Before proving Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 7.3. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>, we require some explicit bases for <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for small <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that Getzler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> showed that <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}=\bigoplus\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}=\bigoplus\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.4</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated graphs form a basis for <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2307.08830v3/pic1.svg" id="S7.Ex4.m1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_markedasmath ltx_img_landscape" width="80" height="50"></object></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the block diagonal pairing against the basis for <math id="S7.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12})</annotation></semantics></math> given by the set of genus <math id="S7.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices decorated by <math id="S7.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{2},\ldots,\omega_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{2},\ldots,\omega_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.5</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="429" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>429</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">429</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated graphs form a basis for <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,13})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,13})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2307.08830v3/13pts.svg" id="S7.Ex5.m1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_markedasmath ltx_img_landscape" width="349" height="89"></object></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Above, <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> are any two markings. In the last group of classes, <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> can be equal to <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Getzler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> proved that <math id="S7.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,13})^{\mathrm{ss}}=429\,\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>429</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,13})^{\mathrm{ss}}=429\,\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>, so it suffices to show that the images of these maps span.
We know that <math id="S7.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,13})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,13})</annotation></semantics></math> is pushed forward from the boundary by Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 2.2. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
Modulo classes of the first kind, the second two kinds are pulled back from <math id="S7.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12})</annotation></semantics></math>. Pulling back the relations from <math id="S7.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12})</annotation></semantics></math>, we can ensure that a fixed marking such as <math id="S7.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> in the first case or <math id="S7.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in the next case is on the genus <math id="S7.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> vertex.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.6</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x,y,1,\ldots,12\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x,y,1,\ldots,12\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math> markings.
The following <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6006" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6006</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6006</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated graphs form a basis for <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,14})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,14})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2307.08830v3/14pts.svg" id="S7.Ex6.m1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_markedasmath ltx_img_landscape" width="548" height="102"></object></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the last grouping above, <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j,</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> can equal <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Getzler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> proved that <math id="S7.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,14})^{\mathrm{ss}}=6006\,\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>6006</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,14})^{\mathrm{ss}}=6006\,\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>, so it suffices to show that the images of these maps span. Modulo classes of the first and second kinds above, the last three kinds are pulled back from <math id="S7.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, using the relations on <math id="S7.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,12})</annotation></semantics></math> we can ensure that a fixed marking among those <math id="S7.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> is on the genus <math id="S7.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> vertex.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.7</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following <math id="S7.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="264" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>264</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">264</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S7.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated graphs form a basis for <math id="S7.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2307.08830v3/12pts.svg" id="S7.Ex7.m1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_markedasmath ltx_img_landscape" width="349" height="87"></object></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Bergström and Faber’s implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> of the Getzler–Kapranov formula in genus <math id="S7.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> shows that, as Galois representations,
<math id="S7.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})^{\mathrm{ss}}=264\,\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>264</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})^{\mathrm{ss}}=264\,\mathsf{L}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, it suffices to show that the above <math id="S7.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="264" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>264</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">264</annotation></semantics></math> maps are independent. We verify this by computing their pullbacks to <math id="S7.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,14})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,14})</annotation></semantics></math> and using Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 7.6. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a> to see that those maps are independent. Indeed, the pullback is given by the graph that replaces the rightmost genus <math id="S7.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> vertex with a genus <math id="S7.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> vertex and adds two markings labeled <math id="S7.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> to that vertex.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.8</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> says that <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the STE generated by <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, it has a graphical presentation, in which the generators are graphs of the sort appearing in Lemmas <a href="#S7.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 7.4. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>–<a href="#S7.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 7.7. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>. In forthcoming work, we will show that <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is also contained in this STE. There, we take a more systematic approach and provide a complete list of relations among the corresponding decorated graph generators.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 7.3. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Figure <a href="#S7.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Proof of Lemma . ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> represents a matrix with <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="891" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>891</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">891</annotation></semantics></math> rows.
We first show that the matrix has rank <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="825" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>825</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">825</annotation></semantics></math> and describe the <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>66</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">66</annotation></semantics></math> relations among the rows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">First notice that the rows in A1 are the only rows with nonzero entries in the first column. Next, rows in A2, A3, and A4 have entries in column 2 that are independent from the other entries that appear in that column for later rows (by Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 7.5. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a>).
This shows that the rows in A1 through A4 are independent and independent from the remaining five rows. Meanwhile, the bottom four rows are block upper triangular. This shows that the rank of the matrix is at least <math id="S7.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="825" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>825</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">825</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2307.08830v3/891rows.png" id="S7.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="648" height="668" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Selected Künneth components of pullbacks of the <math id="S7.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="891" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>891</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">891</annotation></semantics></math> classes to boundary divisors. In the chart, each row represents several rows of a block matrix. The number of rows in each group is listed to its left. The two half-edges glued to make a boundary divisor (pictured across the top) are labeled <math id="S7.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> on the left vertex and <math id="S7.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> on the right vertex.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Meanwhile, it is not hard to check that the following <math id="S7.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>66</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">66</annotation></semantics></math> relations exist among the rows:</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}(ij)+\frac{1}{24}R_{2}(ij)+R_{3}[i,j]+R_{3}[j,i]-R_{4}(ij)+\frac{1}{12}R_{5}(ij)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>24</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>12</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}(ij)+\frac{1}{24}R_{2}(ij)+R_{3}[i,j]+R_{3}[j,i]-R_{4}(ij)+\frac{1}{12}R_{5}(ij)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">To verify these relations one needs to make use of known relations among tautological classes in <math id="S7.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,3})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, let</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2307.08830v3/Mij.svg" id="S7.Ex9.m1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_markedasmath ltx_img_landscape" width="499" height="72"></object></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">We claim <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, consider the
intersection of <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{5}(ik)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{5}(ik)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>. The intersection of <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{5}(ik)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{5}(ik)</annotation></semantics></math> with the first <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> terms in the above equation vanish since there is no <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}</annotation></semantics></math> on a genus <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> vertex with less than <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> markings. Meanwhile, the intersection <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{5}(ij)\cdot R_{5}(ik)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{5}(ij)\cdot R_{5}(ik)</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero using the usual rules for intersecting decorated graphs as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. To see this, one should take the <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> decoration on the <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math> vertex in <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{5}(ij)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{5}(ij)</annotation></semantics></math> to be dual to the decoration used on the <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math> vertex in <math id="S7.SS2.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{5}(ik)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{5}(ik)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, the <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> action on the symbols <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{10}}^{\mathbb{S}_{12}}(V_{1^{10}})=V_{2,1^{10}}\oplus V_{3,1^{9}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ind</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{10}}^{\mathbb{S}_{12}}(V_{1^{10}})=V_{2,1^{10}}\oplus V_{3,1^{9}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a sum of irreducible representations of dimension <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>55</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">55</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that there are either <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>55</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">55</annotation></semantics></math> relations among the <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. There cannot be only <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> relations among the <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> because then there would be more than <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="836" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>836</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">836</annotation></semantics></math> independent copies of <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})</annotation></semantics></math>, violating the dimension count (<a href="#S7.E1" title="In 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>). Hence, there must be exactly <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>55</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">55</annotation></semantics></math> relations among the <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows that the <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="891" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>891</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">891</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> listed on the left of the table generate an <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="836" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>836</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">836</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space, so they span all of <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,12})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.9</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One could instead prove that the <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="891" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>891</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">891</annotation></semantics></math> classes span a space of rank <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="836" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>836</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">836</annotation></semantics></math> by computing the pairing of this space against itself. By the argument above, the pairing must have rank <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="836" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>836</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">836</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, applying Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3. Finite generation ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>(a) together with Lemmas <a href="#S7.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 7.3. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a> and <a href="#S7.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 7.7. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>, and Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Corollary 2.3. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, we obtain one more base case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.10</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,13})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,13})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> lies in the STE generated by <math id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is similar to that of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>. When <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, all odd cohomology vanishes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, so we can assume <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Proposition 2.1]</cite> and the duality between <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{15}^{BM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{15}^{BM}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>15</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that for <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{BM}_{15}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0\text{ for }\begin{cases}15&lt;2g\text{ and }n=0,1\\
15&lt;2g-2+n\text{ and }n\geq 2.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{BM}_{15}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0\text{ for }\begin{cases}15&lt;2g\text{ and }n=0,1\\
15&lt;2g-2+n\text{ and }n\geq 2.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">Combining this with the exact sequence (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6.2. The low degree even homology of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         is tautological ‣ 6. Applications to even cohomology ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>), we reduce inductively to the finitely many cases <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15\geq 2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15\geq 2g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15\geq 2g-2+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15\geq 2g-2+n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. In such cases when <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP, and so by induction and the exact sequence (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6.2. The low degree even homology of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         is tautological ‣ 6. Applications to even cohomology ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>), we reduce to the cases where <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11\leq n\leq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\leq n\leq 14</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11\leq n\leq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\leq n\leq 13</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=11</annotation></semantics></math>, the result follows from Hard Lefschetz and Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 7.1. ‣ 7.1. Thirteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>. When <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12\leq n\leq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\leq n\leq 13</annotation></semantics></math>, the result follows from Lemmas <a href="#S7.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 7.3. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a> and <a href="#S7.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 7.10. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.10</span></a>. For <math id="S7.SS2.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the claim follows from Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 2.2. ‣ 2.2.1. Genus 1 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 7.11</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As Hodge structures or Galois representations, we have</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2d_{g,n}-15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-13}\mathsf{S}_{12}\oplus\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-15}\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2d_{g,n}-15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-13}\mathsf{S}_{12}\oplus\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-15}\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is similar to that of Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Corollary 7.2. ‣ 7.1. Thirteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>, using the additional input that <math id="S7.SS2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\mathsf{S}_{16}\oplus 186263\,\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mn>186263</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\mathsf{S}_{16}\oplus 186263\,\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, p. 491]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p18.1" class="ltx_p">We expect that Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a> can be improved as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 7.12</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The STE generated by <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p19.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> in the next section shows that for <math id="S7.SS2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains no copies of <math id="S7.SS2.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conjecture <a href="#S7.Thmthm12" title="Conjecture 7.12. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.12</span></a> is therefore equivalent to the assertion that the STE generated by <math id="S7.SS2.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S7.SS2.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.SS2.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a></h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first two statements of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> follow immediately from Corollaries <a href="#S6.Thmthm2" title="Corollary 6.2. ‣ 6.2. The low degree even homology of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         is tautological ‣ 6. Applications to even cohomology ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> and <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Corollary 7.2. ‣ 7.1. Thirteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> by Poincaré duality. From Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm11" title="Corollary 7.11. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.11</span></a>, we can similarly conclude that <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of copies of <math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math>. To finish, it suffices to show that when <math id="S8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, no copies of <math id="S8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math> appear.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 8.1</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The STE generated by <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>, as Galois representations or Hodge structures.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>, as Galois representations, there are no copies of <math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}</annotation></semantics></math> by Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Corollary 2.9. ‣ 2.2.2. Genus 2 ‣ 2.2. Pure cohomology in genus 1 and 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>.
This shows that <math id="S8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of copies of <math id="S8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{d_{2,n}-13}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{d_{2,n}-13}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> in the category of Galois representations. But we know that <math id="S8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the STE generated by <math id="S8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a>. By considering the Galois representations involved, it follows that <math id="S8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the STE generated by <math id="S8.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S8.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})^{\mathrm{ss}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of copies of <math id="S8.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{d_{2,n}-13}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{d_{2,n}-13}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>, either as Galois representations or Hodge structures. The second statement follows by Poincaré duality.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To show there are no copies of <math id="S8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, we will use a similar strategy to that of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. We
study the first two maps in the weight <math id="S8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> complex:</p>
<table id="S8.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\xrightarrow{\alpha}\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=1}H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\rightarrow\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=2}(H^{15}\big(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝛼</mo></mover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\xrightarrow{\alpha}\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=1}H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\rightarrow\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=2}(H^{15}\big(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a stable graph of genus <math id="S8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> legs. The first map is the pullback to the normalization of the boundary <math id="S8.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The second map is explicitly described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Section 4.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 8.2</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then the pullback map <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S8.E1" title="In 8. Proof of Theorem ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>) is injective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The map <math id="S8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is injective when <math id="S8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{gr}^{W}_{15}H^{15}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>15</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>15</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{gr}^{W}_{15}H^{15}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Proposition 2.1]</cite>, this holds whenever <math id="S8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;15</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S8.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n\leq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n\leq 15</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP (see Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The result follows from Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4. The Chow–Künneth generation Property ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We now assume <math id="S8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, and we study the second map in (<a href="#S8.E1" title="In 8. Proof of Theorem ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>) as a morphism of Hodge structures. In particular, we consider the <math id="S8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15,0}</annotation></semantics></math> part:</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S8.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=1}H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\xrightarrow{\ \beta\ }\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=2}\big(H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝛽</mo></mover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=1}H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\xrightarrow{\ \beta\ }\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=2}\big(H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p7.1" class="ltx_p">By Lemma <a href="#S8.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 8.2. ‣ 8. Proof of Theorem ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a>, to prove the theorem in the category of Hodge structures, it suffices to show that <math id="S8.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is injective. The domain is a direct sum over graphs with one edge. If <math id="S8.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has one vertex and a loop, then <math id="S8.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})=0</annotation></semantics></math> by induction on <math id="S8.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S8.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has two vertices with an edge connecting them, then by the Künneth formula, we have</p>
<table id="S8.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{a,A\cup p})\oplus H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{b,A^{c}\cup q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{a,A\cup p})\oplus H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{b,A^{c}\cup q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S8.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq b</annotation></semantics></math>. By induction, the above is nonvanishing only if <math id="S8.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A^{c}|\geq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A^{c}|\geq 14</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="S8.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S8.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})=H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q})</annotation></semantics></math> in this case. Let <math id="S8.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the graph obtained from <math id="S8.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> by adding a loop on the genus <math id="S8.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-1</annotation></semantics></math> vertex and decreasing the genus accordingly, as in Figure <a href="#S8.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 8. Proof of Theorem ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Then <math id="S8.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> has a summand <math id="S8.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A^{c}\cup q})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the map <math id="S8.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\rightarrow H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\rightarrow H^{15,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> injects into that summand. This completes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> in the category of Hodge structures.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S8.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2307.08830v3/Gamma2Gamma2p.png" id="S8.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="319" height="85" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>The graph <math id="S8.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> on the left and <math id="S8.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on the right.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S8.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we explain how to deduce the result in the category of Galois representations from the result in the category of Hodge structures. To do so, we need one more lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 8.3</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})</annotation></semantics></math> be the subspace of classes pushed forward from the boundary and let <math id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})</annotation></semantics></math> be the image of <math id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15})\to H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>15</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15})\to H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a direct sum decomposition <math id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})=V\oplus W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})=V\oplus W</annotation></semantics></math>,
as Hodge structures or as Galois representations.
Moreover, we have <math id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\cong\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\cong\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong 186263\,\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>ss</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mn>186263</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\mathrm{ss}}\cong 186263\,\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">There is an exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow V\rightarrow H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\rightarrow W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15})\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow V\rightarrow H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\rightarrow W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15})\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">It thus suffices to show that the composition</span></p>
<table id="S8.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\to W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15})\cong\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\to W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15})\cong\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">is an isomorphism.
The pairing <math id="S8.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\times H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\to\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\times H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\to\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> equips
<math id="S8.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})</annotation></semantics></math> with a nondegenerate bilinear form under which the inclusion of <math id="S8.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is dual to the quotient <math id="S8.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\to W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\to W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15})</annotation></semantics></math>.
To show (<a href="#S8.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 8. Proof of Theorem ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.3</span></a>) is an isomorphism is therefore equivalent to showing that the restriction of the pairing to <math id="S8.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> with itself is full rank. This becomes clear in the category of Hodge structures: Since <math id="S8.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> has type <math id="S8.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13,2}\oplus H^{2,13}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13,2}\oplus H^{2,13}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> has type <math id="S8.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15,0}\oplus H^{0,15}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15,0}\oplus H^{0,15}</annotation></semantics></math>, the pairing between <math id="S8.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, so <math id="S8.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> must be full rank on itself. This claim holds at the level of <math id="S8.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces. Since all the maps above are also maps of Galois representations, we have a direct sum decomposition as Galois representations too.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">The identification of the remainder
<math id="S8.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\mathrm{ss}}=186263\,\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>ss</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>186263</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\mathrm{ss}}=186263\,\mathsf{L}^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>
follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, p. 491]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p11.1" class="ltx_p">By Poincaré duality, to prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, it suffices to show that when <math id="S8.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, there are no copies of <math id="S8.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-15}\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-15}\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math> in the right-hand side of Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm11" title="Corollary 7.11. ‣ 7.2. Fifteenth homology ‣ 7. The thirteenth and fifteenth homology of 
        
          
            
            
              ¯
              M
            
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.11</span></a>. The proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a> exhibits <math id="S8.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2d_{g,n}-15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2d_{g,n}-15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> as a quotient of a direct sum of the form</p>
<table id="S8.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-13}\otimes H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\oplus\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-15}\otimes H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\twoheadrightarrow H^{2d_{g,n}-15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-13}\otimes H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\oplus\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-15}\otimes H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\twoheadrightarrow H^{2d_{g,n}-15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p11.2" class="ltx_p">where the map from each summand on the left is a composition of a forgetful pullback with a gluing pushforward. By Lemma <a href="#S8.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 8.3. ‣ 8. Proof of Theorem ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.3</span></a>, it suffices to show that, for each of these maps, the composition with the inclusion of <math id="S8.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S8.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-15}\otimes W\rightarrow\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-15}\otimes H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\to H^{2d_{g,n}-15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-15}\otimes W\rightarrow\mathsf{L}^{d_{g,n}-15}\otimes H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,15})\to H^{2d_{g,n}-15}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p11.3" class="ltx_p">vanishes.
When we view (<a href="#S8.E4" title="In 8. Proof of Theorem ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.4</span></a>) as a map of Hodge structures, we see that it vanishes since we have already proved Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> in the category of Hodge structures. Thus, (<a href="#S8.E4" title="In 8. Proof of Theorem ‣ Extensions of tautological rings and motivic structures in the cohomology of 
      
        
          
          
            ¯
            M
          
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              g
              ,
              n" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.4</span></a>) vanishes as a map of <math id="S8.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces, and so also as a map of Galois representations.
∎</p>
</div>
</section>
